In this instance the grid contained many shapes, isolated cells, and long edge-aligned runs, which strongly tempted a stamp-copy interpretation. Three demos nevertheless showed only small localized edits: demo 1 changed a subset of color-7 cells to 9; demo 2 changed a subset of color-5 cells to 9; demo 3 changed color-3 cells to 6. The attempted stamp reconstruction was wrong. The demos' input/output dimensions matched, but that did not establish stamping; the actual deltas were the essential evidence.

For future cases, enumerate every delta in each pair before selecting a task class. Compare the geometry, source color, destination color, candidate selector, and unchanged lookalike objects across examples. In particular, do not infer that the long edge bars are the target or template solely from their visual prominence. When the available examples do not yet support a consistent operation, request a demo only with a specific discriminating question; avoid submitting an unvalidated whole-grid guess. This session ended before the transformation rule was resolved, so this note records the reliable diagnostic lesson, not a proposed rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/continual-arc-stream-solving/references/session-lesson-reassess-task-class.md"}
